<BaseLayout title="Electromagnetism" description="Electrostatics, Poisson equations, multipoles, polarization, dielectric response, and boundary conditions for electronic structure." current="theory">
  <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <a href={href('/theory/')}>Theory</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">Electromagnetism</span>
  </nav>

  <h1>Electromagnetism</h1>
  <p class="lede">Electromagnetism supplies the field language behind Coulomb interactions, electrostatic potentials, polarization, dielectric response, charged defects, surfaces, and light–matter coupling.</p>
  <p>The electronic-structure route does not require every topic from a complete electrodynamics course at the same depth. Electrostatics, multipoles, boundary conditions, microscopic and macroscopic fields, and the distinction between longitudinal Coulomb response and transverse electromagnetic radiation carry the highest immediate value.</p>

  <h2>Charge density determines an electrostatic potential only after boundary conditions are stated</h2>
  <p>In SI units, a charge density <math class="math-inline" aria-label="rho of r"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)</annotation></semantics></math> generates a scalar potential through Poisson's equation:</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="laplacian phi equals minus rho over epsilon zero">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><msup><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>φ</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla^2\phi(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)=-\frac&#123;\rho(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)&#125;&#123;\varepsilon_0&#125;.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>The same differential equation supports different physical solutions under isolated, periodic, metallic, dielectric, Dirichlet, Neumann, or mixed boundary conditions. A Poisson solver is therefore part of the physical model, not merely a hidden numerical detail.</p>
  <p>In periodic calculations, the average charge and the zero-wavevector component require special treatment. A charged periodic cell usually implies a compensating convention, finite-size artifacts, and an electrostatic reference that must be reported before absolute energies or potentials are interpreted.</p>

  <h2>The Hartree field is the classical Coulomb part of the electronic problem</h2>
  <p>In atomic units, the Hartree potential generated by the electron density satisfies</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="laplacian v H equals minus four pi n">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><msup><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla^2 v_&#123;\mathrm&#123;H&#125;&#125;(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)=-4\pi n(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;).</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>This term is classical even though the density comes from a quantum problem. Exchange, correlation, self-interaction error, and many-body screening are not contained in the bare Hartree field.</p>
  <p>Long-range Coulomb sums converge conditionally in periodic systems. Ewald methods, reciprocal-space solvers, truncated Coulomb kernels, multipole corrections, and open-boundary solvers implement different assumptions and should not be mixed without checking their reference conventions.</p>

  <h2>Multipoles organize the far field</h2>
  <p>For a localized neutral distribution, the leading electrostatic moment is often the dipole</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="p equals integral r rho of r d cubed r">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">p</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>∫</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mi>r</mi><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;p&#125;=\int \mathbf&#123;r&#125;\rho(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)\,d^3r.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>Monopole, dipole, quadrupole, and higher moments describe progressively finer angular structure in the far field. Their numerical values depend on origin when lower moments are nonzero, and polarization in an infinite periodic solid cannot generally be reduced to a unique dipole moment of one chosen cell.</p>
  <p>Multipole expansions support finite-size corrections, molecular response, defect electrostatics, and boundary matching, but their validity depends on scale separation and localization.</p>

  <h2>Microscopic polarization and macroscopic dielectric response are different levels</h2>
  <p>Macroscopic fields satisfy</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="D equals epsilon zero E plus P">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">D</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi mathvariant="bold">E</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">P</mi><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;D&#125;=\varepsilon_0\mathbf&#123;E&#125;+\mathbf&#123;P&#125;.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>The polarization <math class="math-inline" aria-label="P"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;P&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> is a coarse-grained response variable. The microscopic induced charge and local field can vary strongly within a unit cell, molecule, interface, or heterogeneous dielectric.</p>
  <p>A dielectric tensor can depend on frequency, wavevector, ionic relaxation, temperature, magnetic state, and boundary conditions. Static electronic, static relaxed-ion, and optical dielectric constants are not interchangeable observables.</p>

  <h2>Longitudinal and transverse fields lead to different approximations</h2>
  <p>Electrostatic electronic-structure calculations usually treat the instantaneous longitudinal Coulomb field. Optical propagation and radiation require time-dependent transverse electromagnetic fields and a gauge choice. In many condensed-matter calculations the wavelength is assumed much larger than the simulation cell, producing a spatially uniform external field; this is a dipole or long-wavelength approximation, not the full Maxwell problem.</p>
  <p>Vector potentials, magnetic fields, periodic boundary conditions, and position operators introduce gauge and topology issues. An external-field implementation must therefore be interpreted in its declared gauge, cell geometry, and coupling approximation.</p>

  <h2>Surfaces and interfaces expose electrostatic reference problems</h2>
  <p>Vacuum levels, work functions, band offsets, dipole corrections, slab asymmetry, charged interfaces, and dielectric discontinuities all depend on how the electrostatic potential is averaged and referenced.</p>
  <p>A constant shift in potential does not change forces or an isolated eigenvalue difference, but it matters when comparing separately computed cells. Potential alignment requires a physically comparable reference region or a carefully justified model.</p>

  <h2>Connections through the Atlas</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href={href('/theory/differential-equations/')}>Differential Equations</a> supplies Poisson operators, Green functions, and boundary-value language.</li>
    <li><a href={href('/theory/fourier-analysis/')}>Fourier Analysis</a> explains reciprocal-space Coulomb kernels and the singular zero-wavevector component.</li>
    <li><a href={href('/theory/kohn-sham-density-functional-theory/')}>Kohn–Sham DFT</a> uses the Hartree and external potentials inside the effective one-particle equations.</li>
    <li><a href={href('/theory/linear-response-and-excited-states/')}>Linear Response and Excited States</a> treats dielectric and optical response; <a href={href('/theory/berry-phases-and-electronic-topology/')}>Berry Phases and Electronic Topology</a> treats bulk polarization branches and gauge structure.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Study route</h2>
  <p><a href="https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-02-physics-ii-electricity-and-magnetism-spring-2007/">MIT OpenCourseWare 8.02, <cite>Physics II: Electricity and Magnetism</cite></a> provides the broad undergraduate field foundation. For electronic structure, prioritize electrostatics, Gauss's law, potentials, capacitance and dielectrics, magnetic fields, induction, and Maxwell's equations rather than treating every circuit topic as a prerequisite.</p>
  <p><a href="https://higher.smartedu.cn/course/688543cc16c43a09c00698a5">Northwestern Polytechnical University’s <cite>Electromagnetic Fields and Waves</cite></a>, published through China’s National Smart Education Platform, is a Chinese video course for readers who have vector calculus and introductory electromagnetism. The listed modules cover Maxwell equations, media, boundary conditions, static fields, image methods, separation of variables, and wave propagation; the static-field and boundary sections are especially useful for understanding why a Poisson problem requires a declared geometry and reference. Its later waveguide, radiation, and antenna material is valuable context but does not substitute for a periodic electronic-structure electrostatics or dielectric-response calculation.</p>
  <p><a href="https://vasp.at/wiki/Charged_systems_with_density_functional_theory">VASP Wiki, <cite>Charged systems with density functional theory</cite></a>, together with the official <a href="https://vasp.at/wiki/Category:Electrostatics">Electrostatics documentation index</a>, makes dimensional boundary choices concrete for a current production implementation. The pages distinguish three-dimensional periodic treatments from zero- and two-dimensional Coulomb-kernel truncation, and connect net charge, dipoles, potential references, and correction controls. They are VASP- and version-specific documentation rather than a general derivation or a transferable parameter recipe; the exact installed-version manual and known-issues page must be checked before use.</p>
  <p>The electronic-structure pages then narrow that foundation to Poisson solvers, periodic electrostatics, response functions, polarization, and model-specific boundary conditions.</p>

  <h2>Boundary of this page</h2>
  <p>This page does not prescribe a universal charged-cell correction, potential alignment method, dielectric model, or electromagnetic gauge. It also does not equate a static Poisson solution with a full optical or radiative Maxwell calculation.</p>

  <aside class="review-note">
    <p><strong>Review note.</strong> Scope, Chinese course coverage, and the current VASP electrostatics/charged-system implementation bridge were reviewed through 4 August 2026. Numerical electrostatics remains geometry-, boundary-, code-, version-, and observable-specific.</p>
  </aside>
</BaseLayout>
